{"tag":"MWTB","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-1","file":"","label":"def:DP","number":"2.1","section":"sec-3","order":1,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{def:DP}\n    A random mechanism $f_D$ is said to satisfy $\\epsilon$-differentially private, pure differential privacy, if for all $D\\sim D'$,\n    $$\\log\\left(\\frac{\\int_S d\\mu f_D}{\\int_S d\\mu f_{D'}}\\right)\\leq\\epsilon$$\n    where $\\epsilon$ is a pre-specified parameter referred as the privacy budget, $S$ is any measurable set, and $\\mu$ is the base measure.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","cites_in_head":["dwork2006calibrating"],"labels":["def:DP"],"scope":"paper","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":157,"to":161},"hash":"sha256:47c53b6118c2a00516e86e93880b0f537c7a722f30569f1593047ebdbd282f1f","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"D52W","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-2","file":"","number":"2.2","section":"sec-3","order":2,"title":"Definition","body_md":"A mechanism is said to be $(\\epsilon,\\delta)$-DP if for all adjacent datasets $D\\sim D'$, $$\\int_S d\\mu f_D\\leq\\exp(\\epsilon)\\int_Sd\\mu f_{D'}+\\delta$$\n    where $\\epsilon\u003e0$ and  $\\delta\\in(0,1)$ are pre-specified parameters.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","cites_in_head":["dwork2006our"],"scope":"paper","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":168,"to":171},"hash":"sha256:35e112b7b72d167e90ffa9ba5c63b48528b598a427c5795ef6e688f083e86160","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"UHL7","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-3","file":"","label":"def:Renyi","number":"2.3","section":"sec-3","order":3,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{def:Renyi}\n    For two probability densities $f_1$ and $f_2$, the R\\'enyi divergence of order $\\alpha\u003e1$ from $f_1$ is, $$D_{\\alpha}(P\\|Q)=\\frac{1}{\\alpha-1}\\log \\mathbb{E} \\left(\\frac{f_1(x)}{f_2(x)}\\right)^{\\alpha}.$$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["def:Renyi"],"scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":180,"to":182},"hash":"sha256:a87d72e8f3df9a493b5887afd75e355660461bc2eb610d9af3d5ab2ba65e0ce2","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"23YR","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-4","file":"","label":"def:KL","number":"2.4","section":"sec-3","order":4,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{def:KL}\n    For two probability densities $f_1$ and $f_2$, the Kullback-Leibler divergence from $f_1$ is defined as $$D_{KL}(f_1\\|f_2)=\\int f_1(x)\\log\\frac{f_1(x)}{f_2(x)}dx.$$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["def:KL"],"scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":184,"to":186},"hash":"sha256:393df6f8027959b38ebf1e726caa7f7a5ffb017d9b38407d393f369b14b56dd4","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"KG9Y","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-5","file":"","number":"2.5","section":"sec-3","order":5,"title":"Definition","body_md":"A random mechanism $f_D$ said to satisfy Kullback Leibler DP if\n        $$D_{KL}(f_D\\|f_{D'})\\leq\\alpha$$\n    for all $D\\sim D'$ and a pre-specified $\\alpha$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","cites_in_head":["barber2014privacy","cuff2016differential"],"scope":"paper","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":189,"to":193},"hash":"sha256:5566e01513129325a578a7b4d57ba97918d6521d17e0d08d80c2c186e8ba4caf","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"G841","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-6","file":"","label":"def:CDP","number":"2.6","section":"sec-3","order":6,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{def:CDP}\n    A randomized mechanism $f_D$ is said to satisfy $(\\xi, \\rho)-$zero concentrated differentially privacy if for all $D\\sim D'$ and all $\\alpha\\in(1,\\infty)$, $$D_{\\alpha}(f_D\\|f_{D'})\\leq\\xi+\\rho\\alpha$$ where $D_{\\alpha}(\\cdot\\|\\cdot)$ is the $\\alpha$-R\\'enyi divergence. For $(0,\\rho)$ a mechanism satisfies $\\rho$-zCDP.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","cites_in_head":["bun2016concentrated"],"labels":["def:CDP"],"scope":"paper","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":200,"to":202},"hash":"sha256:2e4a6707373c64e47088f2066b4613fac6436e80ee65fd3d1427327401cb3831","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"4SUV","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-7","file":"","number":"2.7","section":"sec-3","order":7,"title":"Definition","body_md":"A randomized mechanism $f_D$ is said to satisfy $\\zeta-$R\\'enyi differential privacy of order $\\alpha$ $$D_{\\alpha}(f_D\\|f_{D'})\\leq\\zeta$$ for all $D\\sim D'$ and pre-specified $\\zeta$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","cites_in_head":["mironov2017renyi"],"scope":"paper","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":207,"to":209},"hash":"sha256:f986a7ce40be11766a365e4166a48d063265bd61428289a492c7b25c8d024e8f","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"ABUB","kind":"remark","subkind":"example","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"example-1","file":"","label":"ex:Gauss","number":"1","section":"sec-5","order":8,"title":"Example","body_md":"\\label{ex:Gauss}\\cite{rao1945information,costa2015fisher,miyamoto2024closed}\n    Consider the univariate Gaussian density $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi\\sigma^2}}\\exp(-(x-\\mu)^2/(2\\sigma^2))$. Here $\\theta=(\\mu,\\sigma)$ is the set of parameters. \n    The entries of the information matrix are $g_{11}=\\mathbb{E}[\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\mu}\\log f\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\mu}\\log f]=\\mathbb{E}[(x-\\mu)^2/\\sigma^4]=1/\\sigma^4\\mathbb{E}[(\\mu-x)^2]=1/\\sigma^2$, $g_{12}=g_{21}=\\mathbb{E}[\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\mu}\\log f\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\sigma}\\log f]=0$, and $g_{22}=\\mathbb{E}[\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\sigma}\\log f\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\sigma}\\log f]=\\mathbb{E}[-1/\\sigma^2+(x-\\mu)^4/\\sigma^6]=2/\\sigma^2$. We have the information matrix, and hence the Riemannian metric is:   \n    $$g=\\begin{bmatrix}\n        \\frac{1}{\\sigma^2} \u0026 0 \\\\\n        0 \u0026 \\frac{2}{\\sigma^2}\n    \\end{bmatrix}.$$\n    The coordinate pair in the parameter space is $(\\mu,\\sigma)$, and the entries of the metric tensor give us information on how these parameters effect the distance. For instance, we note that $g_{11}$ says that, for two Gaussian densities with the same $\\sigma$, they are increasingly similar for larger $\\sigma.$ This matches intuition as the distributions become infinitely flat as $\\sigma\\rightarrow\\infty$, so they locally become more and more similar. Computing the distance between two normal densities is not immediately straightforward as we need to integrate $$\\int_{(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1)}^{(\\mu_2,\\sigma_2)}\\sqrt{\\sum g_{ij}\\partial\\mu\\partial\\sigma}dt=\\int_{(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1)}^{(\\mu_2,\\sigma_2)}\\sum \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\sigma^2}(\\partial\\mu)^2+\\frac{2}{\\sigma^2}(\\partial\\sigma)^2}dt$$\n    We can identify this line element with that of the upper Poincare half-plane so we have that \n    $$d_R((\\mu_1,\\sigma_1),(\\mu_2,\\sigma_2))=2\\sqrt{2}\\log\\frac{\\sqrt{(\\mu_1-\\mu_2)^2+2(\\sigma_1+\\sigma_2)^2}\n    +\\sqrt{(\\mu_1-\\mu_2)^2+2(\\sigma_1-\\sigma_2)^2}}\n    {\\sqrt{(\\mu_1-\\mu_2)^2+2(\\sigma_1+\\sigma_2)^2}\n    -\\sqrt{(\\mu_1-\\mu_2)^2+2(\\sigma_1-\\sigma_2)^2}}.$$\n    We note this can be algebraically rewritten and include equivalent reformulations in the appendix.\n    The two special cases, as noted by Rao are\n    $d_R((\\mu_1,\\sigma),(\\mu_2,\\sigma))=\\frac{|\\mu_1-\\mu_2|}{\\sigma}$  and   $d_R((\\mu,\\sigma_1),(\\mu,\\sigma_2))=\\sqrt{2}|\\log(\\sigma_2/\\sigma_1)|$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"example","item":"example","head":"environment","counter":"example","number_from":"render","labels":["ex:Gauss"],"scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":258,"to":275},"hash":"sha256:946d224232ffb0caee114b5177a1db86f0bf5eb6d5a1e57c5d084f9dc3f877bb","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-8","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"NTDF","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-8","file":"","number":"3.1","section":"sec-6","order":9,"title":"Definition","body_md":"A random mechanism $f_D$ satisfies $\\theta-$Rao differential privacy if for all adjacent datasets $D\\sim D'$ we have that $$d_R(f_D,f_{D'})\\leq \\theta$$\n    where $d_R(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ is the Rao distance for densities and $\\theta$ is the privacy budget, a pre-specified parameter.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":286,"to":289},"hash":"sha256:a5ab208c555cf83a271e4ea13fc15eb0d0bbf17e66a08cb6b5fb94aa3b992d0e","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-9","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"L1VM","kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"lemma-1","file":"","number":"3.2","section":"sec-7","order":10,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"Given the above, two mechanisms $f_{1,D}$ and $f_{2,D}$ with privacy budgets $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$, respectively, the total privacy budget is $\\sqrt{\\theta_1^2+\\theta_2^2}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":313,"to":315},"hash":"sha256:b7d3aeca5dfbbd86a4d67e5dd7fbb5d5db2959aac1d0e1641b5a6b838de71951","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-10","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"RYIF","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"theorem-1","file":"","label":"thm:RaoPP","number":"3.3","section":"sec-8","order":11,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:RaoPP}\n    Let $f_D$ be a random mechanism that satisfies Rao DP for some $\\theta$ and $\\varphi$ be an arbitrary random function. We have that $\\varphi\\circ f_D$ also satisfies Rao DP under the same $\\theta$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:RaoPP"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":348,"to":350},"hash":"sha256:cc6d1ad0ab4dd1ac791364b42bd5573d1c31578dc36591a64a52842ee353bfc2","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-11","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"176Y","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-9","file":"","label":"def:sens","number":"4.1","section":"sec-9","order":12,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{def:sens}\n    The $\\eta$-sensitivity of a function $h:D\\rightarrow\\mathcal{S}$ and $d_\\eta$ being a distance function is defined $\\Delta_\\eta(h)=\\sup_{D\\sim D'} d_\\eta(h(D),h(D'))$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["def:sens"],"scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":357,"to":359},"hash":"sha256:cbec5c03e2d517afd1a13806ae7403f1633f3a448d49be822678422a9fc49fe9","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-12","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"HBXY","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"theorem-2","file":"","label":"thm:LapDist","number":"4.2","section":"sec-10","order":13,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:LapDist}\n    Let $f_1(x;\\mu_1,\\sigma)$ and $f_2(x;\\mu_2,\\sigma)$ be two Laplace densities with the same scale parameter.                                                             \n    The Rao distance between $f_1$ and $f_2$ is, $$d_R(f_1(x;\\mu_1,\\sigma),f_2(x;\\mu_2,\\sigma))=\\frac{|\\mu_1-\\mu_2|}{\\sigma}.$$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:LapDist"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":367,"to":370},"hash":"sha256:d20655a9dfc6f1a7df5ff37b3f80b6433883cfdc55e45ace28c16a1979bbb3bc","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-13","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"ZNKK","kind":"proof","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"proofsketch-1","file":"","number":"Sketch","section":"sec-10","order":14,"title":"Proof Sketch","body_md":"The proof is nearly identical to Example \\ref{ex:Gauss} with the information matrix being $g_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sigma^2}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proofsketch","item":"proof","head":"environment","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":371,"to":373},"hash":"sha256:cb6e9f51d0f39b1aed3f1a6119e67bc1974de1adadeceff718572f5222aecec3","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-14","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"1G8L","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-10","file":"","number":"4.3","section":"sec-10","order":15,"title":"Definition","body_md":"The Laplace mechanism to release a private version of summary $h(D)$ with the form $f(x;D)=(2\\sigma)^{-1}\\exp\\{|x-h(D)|/\\sigma\\}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":377,"to":379},"hash":"sha256:d539fd56385efd9aa478bbddf3b64ff4eb26f5071d7e2781dfa0a86b97cd1a95","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-15","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"GRGP","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"theorem-3","file":"","label":"thm:LapRao","number":"4.4","section":"sec-10","order":16,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:LapRao}\n    The Laplace mechanism with $\\sigma\\geq\\Delta/\\theta$ satisfies $\\theta$-Rao Privacy.\\\\","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:LapRao"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":383,"to":385},"hash":"sha256:1abc2fef1c28351ef30f3ba7fb72e4fc612f2aaabb0ad036e2b6ab4195bd7771","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-16","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"OX7N","kind":"proof","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"proof-1","file":"","section":"sec-10","order":17,"title":"Proof","body_md":"This follows semi-directly from Theorem \\ref{thm:LapDist}. We have that $d_R(f_D,f_{D'})=\\frac{|h(D)-h(D')|}{\\sigma}$. We thus need $\\frac{|h(D)-h(D')|}{\\sigma}\\leq\\theta$ for all $D\\sim D'$.\n\\begin{align*}\n \\frac{|h(D)-h(D')|}{\\sigma} \u0026\\leq \\frac{\\Delta}{\\sigma} \\\\\n \\implies \\frac{\\Delta}{\\sigma} \u0026\\leq \\theta\\\\\n \\implies \\sigma \u0026\\geq \\frac{\\Delta}{\\theta}. \n\\end{align*}\nThis is the required result.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"theorem-3","attach":3,"span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":386,"to":394},"hash":"sha256:cf88dbf494fece8d8bc3d762721c77e4d88e12ac09d0c5829dffc256086ad1dc","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#proof-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"D1H7","kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"corollary-1","file":"","number":"4.5","section":"sec-10","order":18,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"The Laplace mechanism that satisfies $\\epsilon$ pure DP satisfies Rao DP with $\\theta=\\epsilon$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"corollary","item":"statement/corollary","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":398,"to":400},"hash":"sha256:7860db4bf7c8400d79ad34fbdc2c4543da7598fa5b723a236955eceebc8cb90d","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-17","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"T72R","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"theorem-4","file":"","label":"thm:GaussDist","number":"4.6","section":"sec-11","order":19,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:GaussDist}\n    Let $f_1(x;\\mu_1,\\sigma)$ and $f_2(x;\\mu_2,\\sigma)$ be two Gaussian densities with the same scale parameter. The Rao distance between $f_1$ and $f_2$ is, $$d_R(f_1(x;\\mu_1,\\sigma),f_2(x;\\mu_2,\\sigma))=\\frac{|\\mu_1-\\mu_2|}{\\sigma}.$$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:GaussDist"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":411,"to":413},"hash":"sha256:bda4c353d76a32840c94a745cf22a2c498fc2d9d055d10613c62ab7fde1aa10b","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-18","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"1EM7","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-11","file":"","number":"4.7","section":"sec-11","order":20,"title":"Definition","body_md":"The Gaussian mechanism to release a private version of summary $h(D)$ takes the form $f(x;D)=(2\\pi\\sigma^2)^{-1/2}\\exp\\{(x-h(D))^2/2\\sigma^2\\}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":416,"to":418},"hash":"sha256:2c4f8c4d5b29779a529510d278ab410a75d82b1ffa22a37441dae5f20c8d6ca7","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-19","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"EL4L","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"theorem-5","file":"","number":"4.8","section":"sec-11","order":21,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"The Gaussian mechanism with $\\sigma\\geq\\Delta/\\theta$ satisfies $\\theta$-Rao DP.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":420,"to":422},"hash":"sha256:4b9c2b0365a4472d5e1d778189051e3324a0120855b28a1c754e5f5b1e2234f1","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-20","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"R8N5","kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"corollary-2","file":"","number":"4.9","section":"sec-11","order":22,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"The Gaussian mechanism that satisfies $(\\epsilon,\\delta)$-DP satisfies Rao DP with $\\theta=\\epsilon/\\sqrt{2\\log(1.25/\\delta)}$. The Gaussian mechanism that satisfies $\\mu$-GDP satisfies Rao DP with $\\theta=\\mu$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"corollary","item":"statement/corollary","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":424,"to":426},"hash":"sha256:7f2bfc7c2fff287487bf88c2dd456a186d5af0bd85ad1223c22cc85396203de9","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-21","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"JFN4","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"theorem-6","file":"","label":"thm:GenGaussDist","number":"4.10","section":"sec-12","order":23,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:GenGaussDist}\n    Let $f^N_1(x;\\mu_1,\\sigma)$ and $f^N_2(x;\\mu_2,\\sigma)$ be two Generalized Gaussian densities with the same scale parameter. That is $f_i(x;\\mu,\\sigma)=(2\\sigma\\Gamma(1/N)/N)^{-1}\\exp\\{|x-\\mu_i|^N/\\sigma\\}$. The Rao distance between $f_1$ and $f_2$ is $$d_R(f_1(x;\\mu_1,\\sigma),f_2(x;\\mu_2,\\sigma))=\\frac{|\\mu_1-\\mu_2|}{\\sigma} \\left(\\frac{N\\Gamma(2-1/N)}{\\Gamma(1+1/N)}\\right)^{1/2}$$","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:GenGaussDist"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":434,"to":436},"hash":"sha256:a9211aaedd6cf6fec28282fdaf12ee681fc7447704a193798bfab1a96703e590","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-22","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"HAUK","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-12","file":"","number":"4.11","section":"sec-12","order":24,"title":"Definition","body_md":"The generalized Gaussian mechanism of degree $N$ to release a private version of summary $h(D)$ takes the form $f^N(x;D)=(2\\sigma\\Gamma(1/N)/N)^{-1}\\exp\\{|x-h(D)|^N/\\sigma\\}$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":438,"to":440},"hash":"sha256:9709404ec05de9a4f8081860c498b88c8c262397003d012e7665d811c2990245","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-23","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"QDF1","kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"theorem-7","file":"","label":"thm:GenGaussRao","number":"4.12","section":"sec-12","order":25,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:GenGaussRao}\n    The Generalized Gauss mechanism with $\\sigma\\geq\\Delta \\left(\\frac{N\\Gamma(2-1/N)}{\\Gamma(1+1/N)}\\right)^{1/2}/\\theta$ satisfies $\\theta$-Rao Privacy.\\\\","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"theorem","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","labels":["thm:GenGaussRao"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":442,"to":444},"hash":"sha256:fcaa9dab4bb911e91e13ebf5428262eff3b941174108ec88576dc4d2d8287186","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-24","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"KC0N","kind":"proof","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"proof-2","file":"","section":"sec-15","order":26,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Let $\\varphi$ be an arbitrary deterministic function and $f_D$ be our mechanism. By definition we have $ d_R(f_D,f_{D'})\\leq\\theta$. We have that,\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\int_\\mathcal{X} \\varphi(f(x;D))+\\varphi(f(x;D')) dx\n        \u0026= \\int_{\\varphi^{-1}(\\mathcal{X}) }f(x;D)+f(x;D') dx. \\\\\n                                           \n    \\end{align*}\n    Thus we have that\n    \\begin{align*}\n        \\int \\left( \\sqrt{\\varphi(f(x;D))}+\\sqrt{\\varphi(f(x;D'))}\\right)^2 - 2\\int \\sqrt{\\varphi(f(x;D))\\varphi(f(x;D'))}  \u0026=  \\int \\left( \\sqrt{f(x;D)}+\\sqrt{f(x;D')}\\right)^2 \\\\\n        \u0026- 2\\int \\sqrt{f(x;D)(f(x;D')} \\\\\n         \\int \\sqrt{\\varphi(f(x;D))\\varphi(f(x;D'))}  \u0026=   \\int \\sqrt{f(x;D)f(x;D')} \\leq \\theta\n    \\end{align*}\n    Note that $(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})^2-2\\sqrt{xy}=x+y$.\n    Since this holds for all $D\\sim D'$ we have by symmetry that $\\|\\varphi(f(x;D))-\\varphi(f(x;D')) \\|\n       \\leq  \\|f(x;D)-f(x;D')  \\|$.                                                                                                                        \n       \n       We thus have the required result.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","flags":["appendix","orphan"],"span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":500,"to":519},"hash":"sha256:4c0f10c68f01006159f31ace31437a701149a59044b365db06440804eca233fb","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#proof-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"tag":"159P","kind":"definition","paper":"2508.17135","version":"1","local":"definition-13","file":"","number":"C.1","section":"sec-17","order":27,"title":"Definition","body_md":"A mechanism $f_D$ is said to satisfy $\\mu$-Gaussian differential privacy if for all adjacent datasets $D\\sim D'$ $$T(f_D,f_{D'})\\geq G_{\\mu}$$ where $ G_{\\mu}=:T(N(0,1),N(\\mu,1))$, $T(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ is a trade-off function, and $\\mu$ is the privacy parameter.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"definition","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"theorem","number_from":"render","scope":"paper","origin":"own","flags":["appendix"],"span":{"file":"main_arxiv.tex","from":526,"to":528},"hash":"sha256:933af85767709449cdc040950c0ce6be845e9991d680b9712c0bfb75fe1f380c","minhash":"","census":"2508.17135v1#env-25","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
